WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.355
Picture a small bank at the end of the day.

2
00:00:05.355 --> 00:00:11.035
The cashier writes the closing balance in the ledger, 12,000, and the number of entries, 41.

3
00:00:11.035 --> 00:00:16.160
Then she locks the ledger in its cabinet, and leaves the key for the night manager.

4
00:00:16.367 --> 00:00:21.382
When the night manager opens that cabinet with that key, she can trust what she reads.

5
00:00:21.382 --> 00:00:24.764
Everything written before the cabinet was locked is there.

6
00:00:24.967 --> 00:00:27.780
Threads need the same kind of handover.

7
00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:32.974
Make the cashier a thread, T1, and the night manager another thread, T2.

8
00:00:32.974 --> 00:00:36.436
T1 writes two plain fields, balance and entries.

9
00:00:36.436 --> 00:00:37.879
T2 reads them later.

10
00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:39.524
But later isn't enough.

11
00:00:39.524 --> 00:00:45.406
In the last lesson, main set a stop flag, and seconds later the worker still saw the old value.

12
00:00:45.406 --> 00:00:49.183
Clocks are great for meetings, and no help at all to threads.

13
00:00:49.183 --> 00:00:52.527
A read is sure to see a write only through a handover.

14
00:00:52.733 --> 00:00:54.300
Java's cabinet is a lock.

15
00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:58.623
Every Java object carries one, and the keyword synchronized takes it.

16
00:00:58.623 --> 00:01:03.134
Leaving a synchronized block is locking the cabinet and leaving the key.

17
00:01:03.134 --> 00:01:08.397
Entering a synchronized block on the same lock is opening the cabinet with that key.

18
00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:10.098
Watch the handover.

19
00:01:10.098 --> 00:01:12.699
T1 writes, then leaves the block.

20
00:01:12.699 --> 00:01:16.798
T2 enters on the same lock, and reads 41 and 12,000.

21
00:01:16.798 --> 00:01:23.261
Everything T1 wrote before it left, T2 sees after it enters, even in plain fields.

22
00:01:23.467 --> 00:01:26.757
Now a question that sounds too silly to ask out loud.

23
00:01:26.757 --> 00:01:27.812
A lock is a lock.

24
00:01:27.812 --> 00:01:30.420
Does it matter which one the reader takes?

25
00:01:31.133 --> 00:01:32.500
Here's a real test.

26
00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:37.463
The writer sets balance to 12,000 inside synchronized on LEDGER_LOCK.

27
00:01:37.463 --> 00:01:44.368
The reader spins, and on every pass, it locks a brand-new object of its own, then reads balance.

28
00:01:44.368 --> 00:01:46.669
Does the reader ever see 12,000?

29
00:01:49.667 --> 00:01:49.885
No.

30
00:01:49.885 --> 00:01:52.862
Three seconds later, it's still spinning.

31
00:01:52.862 --> 00:01:58.963
Now the reader locks LEDGER_LOCK, the writer's own lock, and it sees 12,000 at once.

32
00:01:58.963 --> 00:02:00.561
Same field, same loop.

33
00:02:00.561 --> 00:02:02.159
Only the lock changed.

34
00:02:02.367 --> 00:02:06.238
A brand-new object is a cabinet nobody else will ever open.

35
00:02:06.238 --> 00:02:12.799
Locking it is like buying a new padlock every morning, locking it to nothing, and feeling very safe.

36
00:02:12.799 --> 00:02:20.541
The JIT even notices that no one else can take that lock, removes it, and reads balance once, just like the stop flag.

37
00:02:21.367 --> 00:02:25.161
There's a second kind of handover, one that makes nobody wait.

38
00:02:25.161 --> 00:02:29.629
When the cashier is done, she hangs a sign on the cabinet: ledger closed.

39
00:02:29.629 --> 00:02:35.381
Anyone who reads the sign and sees closed is promised everything she wrote before she hung it.

40
00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:38.100
In Java, the sign is a volatile field.

41
00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:40.929
Hanging it is a write: closed becomes true.

42
00:02:40.929 --> 00:02:43.955
Reading the sign is a read of that same field.

43
00:02:43.955 --> 00:02:48.100
No key, no waiting, and any number of readers can look at once.

44
00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:51.719
A volatile field works like a lock without the waiting.

45
00:02:51.933 --> 00:02:53.990
And the word same matters here too.

46
00:02:53.990 --> 00:02:57.811
A write to one volatile field and a read of another link nothing.

47
00:02:57.811 --> 00:03:02.748
That's the night manager trusting the ledger because the broom cupboard says closed.

48
00:03:04.267 --> 00:03:05.159
Now the names.

49
00:03:05.159 --> 00:03:08.665
Locking the cabinet, or hanging the sign, is a release.

50
00:03:08.665 --> 00:03:12.299
Opening with the key, or reading the sign, is an acquire.

51
00:03:12.299 --> 00:03:15.806
And inside one thread, each step comes before the next.

52
00:03:15.806 --> 00:03:17.591
That's called program order.

53
00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:18.836
Chain the links.

54
00:03:18.836 --> 00:03:20.909
Write the balance, then release.

55
00:03:20.909 --> 00:03:23.952
The same lock's acquire, then read the balance.

56
00:03:23.952 --> 00:03:27.126
The memory model calls this chain happens-before.

57
00:03:27.126 --> 00:03:31.529
The write of balance happens-before its read, so T2 must see 12,000.

58
00:03:31.733 --> 00:03:36.063
The cabinet picture breaks in one place: nothing is carried anywhere.

59
00:03:36.063 --> 00:03:38.384
Both threads look at the same memory.

60
00:03:38.384 --> 00:03:41.773
Without a handover, a reader often does see the write.

61
00:03:41.773 --> 00:03:43.216
It just has no promise.

62
00:03:44.033 --> 00:03:48.022
Every one of these rules says same, and that's where the bugs live.

63
00:03:48.022 --> 00:03:49.986
Two different locks link nothing.

64
00:03:49.986 --> 00:03:56.416
A synchronized setter with a plain getter links nothing: the writer releases, and the reader never acquires.

65
00:03:56.416 --> 00:03:58.975
A lock on a brand-new object links nothing.

66
00:03:58.975 --> 00:04:01.773
And two different volatile fields link nothing.

67
00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:09.004
So for every field two threads share, check both sides, and name the one lock or the one volatile field they share.

68
00:04:09.833 --> 00:04:12.995
In code, Java on the left and Kotlin on the right.

69
00:04:12.995 --> 00:04:17.612
One lock object, LEDGER_LOCK, and both the writer and the reader take it.

70
00:04:18.433 --> 00:04:21.739
So, does it matter which lock the reader takes?

71
00:04:21.739 --> 00:04:22.513
Completely.

72
00:04:22.513 --> 00:04:27.577
A lock or a volatile field links only the threads that use the same one.

73
00:04:27.577 --> 00:04:29.195
Same key, same cabinet.

74
00:04:29.195 --> 00:04:30.883
Same sign, same promise.

75
00:04:31.100 --> 00:04:32.040
Quick check.

76
00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:35.329
T1 writes a volatile field called version.

77
00:04:35.329 --> 00:04:40.028
T2 reads a volatile field called checksum, then the balance.

78
00:04:40.028 --> 00:04:41.751
Is T2 promised 12,000?
